npm-packages/
# Root tests/ holds Playwright specs, Node E2E and node-based unit tests - none
# of it is Meteor code, and Meteor's file watcher otherwise churns on the
# tests/playwright/test-results/ tree (inotify errors). The leading slash anchors
# this to the repo root only, so it does NOT match server/lib/tests/ or
# client/lib/tests/ (the Mocha suites, loaded via meteor.testModule in
# package.json). A naked "tests/" would match a dir named tests at any depth and
# would hide those suites, making `meteor test` report "0 passing".
/tests/

# Build/tool scratch space: a full Node 24 install (headers for EVERY openssl arch),
# a Go toolchain with its module/build caches, build logs and repro dirs. 20,535
# DIRECTORIES, none of it app code. Meteor's watcher takes one inotify watch per
# directory, and the per-user limit (fs.inotify.max_user_watches, 65536 here) is
# shared with every other watcher the user runs - editors included. Watching this
# tree is what made `meteor` fail with
#   inotify_add_watch on '.tools/node-.../openssl/archs/BSD-x86_64/asm_avx2'
#   failed: No space left on device
# ENOSPC from inotify_add_watch means "watch limit reached", NOT a full disk.
/.tools/

# The FerretDB v1 fork checkout: a complete Go project + its docs, 9,148 more
# directories. Nothing in WeKan imports from it - the FerretDB binary is
# downloaded from its GitHub releases at build time - so Meteor has no reason to
# watch or scan it. Both are already in .gitignore, but the watcher only honours
# .meteorignore.
/FerretDB/

# ── The other checkouts that have accumulated beside the app ─────────────────
#
# Everything below is the SAME problem as /.tools/ and /FerretDB/ above, and it
# is why `meteor build` ran out of memory: Meteor scans the app directory, and
# these are in .gitignore only, which the scanner does not read.
#
# The count is what makes it fatal. Meteor builds one ignore matcher per
# directory it descends into, each carrying the whole accumulated pattern list,
# and a heap snapshot taken at the moment the build died showed 945 of those
# matchers holding 14.3 million rule objects - 980 MB of IgnoreRule plus 667 MB
# of sliced strings, 308 MB of concatenated strings and 274 MB in 945 copies of
# one 296 KB pattern list: about 2.5 GB of the 4.1 GB heap, and still only 945
# directories in. There are 6,867 directories under this one, of which 5,860 are
# in the trees named here; WeKan's own code is the remaining ~1,000. So the cost
# was very nearly SEVEN TIMES what the snapshot caught, which is why raising
# --max-old-space-size never helped - the ceiling was not the variable.
#
# Excluding them takes the scan back to WeKan itself.

# The Node.js fork checkout (github.com/wekan/node): 4,132 directories, 45,527
# files, 2.3 GB - by far the largest of these. WeKan uses its RELEASE BINARIES,
# downloaded per architecture at build time; the source is here to build them,
# and nothing in the app imports it.
/node/

# The mongo-tools checkout: another Go project, 738 directories. Same story -
# WeKan ships the built binaries, not this source.
/mongo-tools/

# TSC (the game) and its website, cloned here for the release work. 472 and 512
# directories, 810 MB together, and not WeKan at all.
/TSC/
/secretchronicles.github.io/

# Two more WeKan checkouts, 296 directories each. These are worse than the rest:
# they contain client/, server/ and models/, so they are not merely scanned -
# Meteor loads server/ and client/ EAGERLY, and it would be pulling a second and
# third copy of the whole app into this build.
/wekan-gantt-gpl/
/wekan-ondra/

# DO NOT ADD /_build/ OR /_build-local-test/ HERE. They look like build output
# that should be ignored - they are gitignored, and they are the first two
# entries of the 296 KB pattern list above - and excluding them breaks the
# build outright:
#
#   error: Could not find mainModule for 'os' architecture:
#   _build/main-prod/server-meteor.js
#   Check the "meteor" section of your package.json file?
#
# They are not leftovers, they are the HANDOFF. rspack compiles the app into
# _build/main-prod/ (and _build/test/ for a test run), and Meteor then reads
# _build/main-prod/server-meteor.js and client-meteor.js as the application's
# main modules - package.json names client/main.js and server/main.js, and the
# rspack integration repoints mainModule at its own output. Ignoring them hides
# the files Meteor is about to be handed. They cost 3 directories each, so there
# was nothing to win here anyway.

# Local build and test logs. WEKAN_LOG_ROOT prefers ../log/<datetime>/, but
# falls back to ./log when the parent is not writable - which is exactly the
# case inside the Flatpak sandbox, so this directory does appear here.
/log/
